Exercise 1.13 asks to verify every assertion made about the system \(B\), the infinite form of Example 1.4, and to draw the binary tree of all finite bit strings \(\sigma \in \Sigma^*\) with the neighbourhoods, the approximation order, and the total elements, which sit as limit nodes along the top. The imported stems build those limit nodes for one path at a time. `prefixSeq p n` is the length-\(n\) initial segment of a path \(p : \mathbb{N} \to \mathrm{Bool}\), the empty list at stage \(0\) and the previous segment with the next bit appended; `prefixSeq_length` is the induction that this list has length \(n\), `prefixSeq_prefix_succ` is the one-step prefix, and `prefixSeq_mono` inducts along \(n \le m\) to obtain the prefix relation. `branchSeq p n` is the finite element of that prefix, `branchSeq_mono` turns the prefix relation into the approximation order, and `branch p` is the ascending union of that monotone sequence. `branch_mem_iff` says the cone of \(\sigma\) lies on the branch exactly when \(\sigma\) is a prefix of some finite initial segment, by the union lemma for a monotone sequence in both directions. `prefix_comparable_of_mem` says that if one element contains the cones of \(\sigma\) and of \(\tau\), then one string is a prefix of the other: the intersection of the cones belongs to the element, so some cone is contained in it, trichotomy gives an inclusion or disjointness, and disjointness would put the generating string of the contained cone into the empty set. `branch_isTotal`, exported as `example_113`, concludes that the branch is maximal: if an element lies above it and contains the cone of \(\sigma\), the cone of the path prefix of the same length also lies in that element, the two prefixes are comparable, and equal lengths force the strings to coincide when the path prefix extends \(\sigma\). That shows each infinite path determines a total element. The stems leave unproved the remaining assertions about \(B\): that the cones \(\sigma\Sigma^*\) form a neighbourhood system, that \(\sigma x\) is an element, that the finite elements are the principal filters \(\sigma\bot\), that \(\sigma_0\bot\) approximates \(\sigma_1\bot\) exactly when \(\sigma_0\) is an initial segment of \(\sigma_1\), and that an arbitrary element is the union of a chain of such finite elements; they also draw no picture. **Theorem:** partial — each infinite path is shown to determine a total element, which is the limit-node part of the exercise, and the other assertions about \(B\) are absent from the imported stems. **Proof:** partial — comparability of prefixes of equal length is a complete maximality proof for `branch p`, and that argument stops at the total elements of infinite paths.
